The fix is complete. Here's what I changed and why:

**Root cause.** In `poll`, the very first thing checked after the "packet already pending" guard is whether `state->exception` is set (line 458). For an `UNKNOWN_TABLE` error, the exception is caught inside `sendQuery` (lines 363–377) during query analysis — *before* `poll` is ever called. So on the first `poll`, `state->exception` is already populated, and the original code immediately set `next_packet_type = Protocol::Server::Exception` and returned. Any log messages buffered in `state->logs_queue` during analysis were never drained, so the client only ever saw the exception.

Notably, the later exception block at lines ~507–514 *already* did the right thing (`if (needSendLogs()) return true;`) — but that block is only reached when the exception is thrown during pipeline execution inside `poll` itself. The early-set case bypassed it entirely.

**The fix.** The early `state->exception` branch now calls `needSendLogs` first:

```cpp
if (state->exception)
{
    /// Flush any buffered log messages before delivering the exception,
    /// so that logs accumulated before the failure are not discarded.
    if (needSendLogs())
        return true;

    next_packet_type = Protocol::Server::Exception;
    return true;
}
```

`needSendLogs` drains the queue into a block and sets `next_packet_type = Protocol::Server::Log`, returning `true`. The client consumes the `Log` packet (resetting `next_packet_type`) and polls again; this repeats until the queue is empty, at which point `needSendLogs` returns `false` and the `Exception` packet is finally delivered. This mirrors the existing execution-path handling exactly, so `Debug`/`Trace` lines now appear on stderr ahead of the `UNKNOWN_TABLE` error.

The change is confined to `src/Client/LocalConnection.cpp`; no test files were touched. Note per the repo convention I haven't committed anything to `master` — let me know if you'd like this on a branch/committed.

